feov-record — the red merge seat — the board's only writer.

If you need a verb or a flag that is not listed here, it does not exist for you:
do not improvise around it, and do not hand-write the artifact. Record what you
needed and what you would have done with your role's 'friction' verb — a missing
capability is a finding about the tooling, and that channel is how it gets fixed.

Usage:
  feov-record [flags]
  feov-record [command]

Available Commands:
  carry           restate an earlier round's closure, when the work is done and you are not re-attesting it
  close           close a gap with the verification you did THIS round, naming what you checked it against
  closing         your closing argument on one docketed gap, when the bench is about to rule on it
  completion      Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
  count-claims    count the FOOTNOTED declarative claims in blue's report (read-only)
  fetch           cached, hash-verified web read (replaces WebFetch); serves both sides the same bytes
  friction        a capability gap or protocol misfit — and the explicit empty form, for the sittings where nothing blocked you
  help            Help about any command
  inquiry-support your per-round vote on whether the report still carries a direction blue proposed
  mint            turn lens findings into a graded gap on the board, when a defect is real and belongs there
  near-match      screen a candidate against the board BEFORE minting, so a reopen does not arrive as a fresh gap
  position        your round narrative — the argument the other side answers, rendered as this round's RED section
  register        your first act at the seat, and the one call that needs your seat id — nothing else runs until it does
  regrade         move a grade on a gap that already exists, with the reason it moved
  spot-check      sample the closure archive — a duty every round owes, including the rounds with nothing to sample
  verdict         the seat's terminal act: the PASS or FAIL, and the checkpoint that follows it

Command groups — each holds commands THIS page does not list:
  class           the gap-class registry: what KINDS of defect this run recognises
  close           close a gap WITH its verification: --id R2-3 --as closed|closed_with_regression|... --verified-by L1 --verified-with "git show" --verified-against "7bc501e:path" [--superseded-by R3-1] --reason "<what was verified and why it holds>"
  closing         a ### RED CLOSING entry per docketed gap: --id <gap> --reason <prose>
  friction        a capability gap or protocol misfit, as an event that survives aborts: --reason. CLOSE THIS CHANNEL EVERY SITTING: --none --reason "<what you reached for and found>" says nothing blocked you, which silence cannot say — an empty friction log reads the same whether the sitting was clean or the channel went unused
  inquiry-support your per-round read of the REPORT against the lines of inquiry on the record: --reason "<what the report says at those lines>". Read the report ONCE, cover every line in the one act — and where a line's treatment is thin, missing or unsupported by the text, MINT A GAP for it. This event records only that the read happened; the shortfalls it finds are ordinary defects. A PASS is refused until it exists.
  mint            mint a board gap (id is TOOL-assigned; --key <stable-label> makes retries idempotent): --class <slug> --quote "<the exact sentence it lives at>" --problem "..."|--reason --fix "..." --check "<acceptance check red runs at re-audit>" --severity/--likelihood/--impact/--complexity <grade> [--supersedes R1-2,R1-7] [--found-by L5-F3,L6-F2]
  near-match      screen a candidate against the board before minting: --problem "<what is wrong>" [--quote "<the sentence it lives at>"] — returns the top 5 gaps (open AND closed) by lexical overlap, so a near-duplicate surfaces as a reopen (mint --supersedes <id>) rather than a fresh gap. The tool SCREENS and ranks; you decide reopen-or-new. Read-only: it records nothing.
  position        the round's ### RED section (prose via --reason)
  register        FIRST ACTION at the seat: `register`, with no flags — the engine injects your run and your identity, and a --run or --seat-id that disagrees with the dispatch is refused rather than obeyed
  regrade         same-id grade movement, recorded with its reason: --id R2-5 [--severity/--likelihood/--impact/--complexity <grade>] --reason "..."
  show            read a projection on STDOUT (the tool is the read path; the .md files are for human verification). With no projection named, you get YOUR PENDING WORK: report | board | findings | work | motions | debate | changes | evidence | lines-of-inquiry | telemetry
  spot-check      the round archive spot-check record (W1.8 duty): --ids R1-4,R2-7 --reason "<what the sample showed>" | --none --reason "<why there was nothing to sample>" when the archive was empty at round start
  verdict         the seat's terminal act: --as PASS|FAIL — checkpoints records/ to the recovery mirror

Flags:
  -h, --help               help for feov-record
      --json               emit a structured JSON result (and structured errors) instead of human text
      --run string         the run directory. The PreToolUse hook injects it in a real run, which is why you rarely type it. A value that DISAGREES with the run you were dispatched into is refused rather than obeyed
      --seat-id register   your seat id, as the dispatch prompt states it (SEAT_ID). You pass it ONCE, at register, which binds it to you on the record; after that every call resolves it for you and typing it is optional. It SELECTS this surface — the verbs listed are the ones your seat may run — and a value disagreeing with what you registered as is refused rather than obeyed
  -v, --version            version for feov-record

Use "feov-record [command] --help" for more information about a command.

